			<div class="content" id="post-content-3714443">
									

<p>This skip was a bit borderline for me. Tentatively +1-ing, but I think you could make this a lot snappier (maybe after the contest?).</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Feedback</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>On one hand, the central anomaly is <em>very</em> interesting &#8212; and the analogue you're drawing is <em>very</em> compelling. The single line you use to imply what's going on is suitably creepy (though I wish you could have found a way to indicate <em>portions</em> of persons, rather than whole persons &#8212; as a 'whole person' is neither a material nor entirely edible).</p>
<p>(As an aside, minor gripe &#8212; this is the incorrect use of 'individual'. That being said, so many people use it the way you're using that <em>merely the act of pointing this out</em> qualifies me as an absurdly pedantic dickhead)</p>
<p>On the other hand, I was semi-surprised that you didn't structure it so the Foundation becomes the British analogue (IE, imply that this is a Foundation-created anomaly resulting from their cruel treatment of an indigenous group and the ensuing attempt to erase all information regarding that treatment). For example, your use of '[DATA LOST]' rather than '[DATA EXPUNGED]', which implies that the Foundation is dealing with the consequences of erasure &#8212; rather than engaging in it themselves.</p>
<p>The main thing that made my upvote tentative, though, was the prose: It's clunky in a lot of places. For example:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-3158 is a tropical island, located 42 kilometers off the coast of ███████, with an area of 4.8km<sup>2</sup>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This reads so much more cleanly if you shift that middle clause to the end:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-3158 is a tropical island with an area of 4.8km<sup>2</sup> located 42 kilometers off the coast of ███████.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It also shifts focus to the censor bar (since that's now the end of the sentence), which is important &#8212; because censoring and erasing is (at least in part) what this skip is all about.</p>
<p>Other examples:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>All anomalous effects cease once the subject leaves SCP-3158.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>There's only one anomalous effect so far; why not just say 'This effect ceases once the subject leaves SCP-3158'?</p>
<p>(as an aside, while I'm here, I will say that the decision to end this sentence &#8212;)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Affected subjects will prefer not to acknowledge the presence of blood in their eyes, attempting to refocus any such discussion on SCP-3158's natural beauty, calmness, or <strong>exoticism</strong>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>(&#8212;with 'exoticism' was an excellent choice; it immediately evokes fetishization of indigenous cultures. As soon as I saw that word, I understood what you were going for)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The blood does not match the genetic profile of the subject, but rather, that of [DATA LOST].</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This seems oddly clumsy; why not just:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The blood matches the genetic profile of [DATA LOST].</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8212; since by saying this, we've successfully clarified the blood doesn't match the profile of the subject?</p>
<p>(This isn't a criticism, but I'd also like to point out that this is also an excellent opportunity to use a word like 'Excreted' or 'Secreted'. IE, 'Excreted blood matches the genetic profile of [DATA LOST]' &#8212; which, <em>all by its lonesome</em>, is an incredibly creepy sentence!)</p>
<p>Etc &#8212; I won't go through the whole thing like this; I think you get the idea. The reason I'm bringing this all up is because this skip is <em>super short</em>, and I feel like the shorter your skip is, the more important it is that your prose be clean, polished, and <em>full of punch</em>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>(For reference, I'm trying to be a bit more discerning with my upvotes &#8212; and give more feedback when I vote the way I do. This is kind of a new deal for me, so if any of the above comes off as clumsy or undesired, I apologize!)</p>
<p>Anyway, I thought this was a pretty great idea &#8212; good luck with the contest!</p>

